WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.492 --> 00:00:04.525
Find a bigger stage.

00:00:04.921 --> 00:00:06.701
A challenge for a lot of entrepreneurs

00:00:06.701 --> 00:00:08.611
is the product or service that you’re selling

00:00:09.124 --> 00:00:12.291
isn’t really all that great or different.

00:00:12.291 --> 00:00:14.298
It’s just average, it’s boring

00:00:14.298 --> 00:00:15.720
and it’s one of the main reasons why

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.593
80% of companies will fail.

00:00:17.765 --> 00:00:20.413
80% of new businesses will fail in the first 5 years

00:00:20.831 --> 00:00:22.175
because your stuff is boring,

00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:23.573
it’s just okay,

00:00:23.573 --> 00:00:24.791
it’s average.

00:00:25.131 --> 00:00:27.801
What you need to do first is find something that’s great

00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:29.135
something that you can excel at

00:00:29.135 --> 00:00:30.469
and be one of the best in the world at

00:00:30.469 --> 00:00:31.805
and when you find that

00:00:32.053 --> 00:00:34.584
and for those of you entrepreneurs who are on that path

00:00:34.584 --> 00:00:35.940
who are doing something important

00:00:36.324 --> 00:00:39.313
who poured your passion into this business that you’re creating

00:00:39.597 --> 00:00:41.670
and you’re not just creating average, okay

00:00:41.706 --> 00:00:44.007
get by products and services

00:00:44.227 --> 00:00:46.784
what you’re doing is going to change the world;

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.206
when you get to that point then you need to find a bigger stage.

00:00:50.485 --> 00:00:51.857
You need to let the world know

00:00:51.857 --> 00:00:53.229
about what you’re doing

00:00:53.589 --> 00:00:54.961
and how good you are.

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:57.007
One of my favorite stories is of Eminem.

00:00:57.007 --> 00:00:59.235
And when he was first getting started

00:00:59.235 --> 00:01:01.707
he had a tough upbringing, he came from a broken home

00:01:01.871 --> 00:01:02.992
his parents weren’t together

00:01:02.992 --> 00:01:05.213
he lived with various members of his family

00:01:05.213 --> 00:01:07.156
bounced around from state to state.

00:01:07.156 --> 00:01:10.557
He pursued rap because he loved doing it

00:01:10.587 --> 00:01:12.483
but he couldn’t make any money off of it.

00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:14.577
He took jobs at restaurants

00:01:14.577 --> 00:01:17.461
to just make ends meet.

00:01:17.581 --> 00:01:19.935
But he thought to himself, ‘You know what – I’m really good.

00:01:19.935 --> 00:01:21.227
I want to make a business out of this.

00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:23.283
I want to become a professional rapper.

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:25.780
Even though at the time there were no white rappers, right.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.042
People looked at him and laughed.

00:01:27.042 --> 00:01:29.351
“You’re white. You’re rapping? What are you, crazy?”

00:01:29.724 --> 00:01:31.525
And he felt he was good enough

00:01:31.525 --> 00:01:33.720
that he needed to find a bigger stage.

00:01:34.131 --> 00:01:36.002
And his bigger stage moment came

00:01:36.002 --> 00:01:39.473
in 1997 at the Rap Olympics in Los Angeles.

00:01:40.104 --> 00:01:41.844
When he went there he was dead broke.

00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:44.132
He had nothing. He had no money.

00:01:44.535 --> 00:01:48.878
He was evicted from his house the day before and that night

00:01:48.945 --> 00:01:51.158
he had to break into his house

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:53.432
sleep on the floor just so he had a place to sleep

00:01:53.432 --> 00:01:55.576
before going to LA for the Rap Olympics.

00:01:55.576 --> 00:01:56.778
Everything was on the line for him

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.520
and he went to the Rap Olympics in LA

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.902
and he won second place.

00:02:01.174 --> 00:02:02.378
And that was all he needed.

00:02:02.401 --> 00:02:03.736
That was the turning point.

00:02:03.736 --> 00:02:07.417
He went from poorly bankrupt broke, everybody laughed at him

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.966
to now being

00:02:10.216 --> 00:02:10.942
noticed.

00:02:11.352 --> 00:02:13.940
And Dr. Dre who was a producer

00:02:14.493 --> 00:02:17.263
looked at him, listened to his tapes and said

00:02:17.263 --> 00:02:18.099
“I want to sign this guy.

00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:20.357
” People said“Dre, you’re crazy. Dude’s white.”

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:21.994
He said, “I don’t care if he’s purple.

00:02:22.387 --> 00:02:24.682
If he can rap I want to be working with him.”

00:02:24.883 --> 00:02:26.641
And that was the start of Eminem’s career.

00:02:27.142 --> 00:02:29.887
He put all this work, effort, passion

00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:34.176
love into making his rap something meaningful

00:02:35.359 --> 00:02:36.592
but it only really took off

00:02:36.745 --> 00:02:39.037
when he found his bigger stage at the Rap Olympics.

00:02:40.191 --> 00:02:41.596
So if you’re really good at what you do

00:02:41.943 --> 00:02:44.768
if you love doing it, if you’re creating something special

00:02:44.888 --> 00:02:47.365
you’re not just selling an average product or service

00:02:47.671 --> 00:02:49.106
there’s something unique about what you’re doing

00:02:49.106 --> 00:02:51.565
and it’s really making an impact on people’s lives

00:02:52.493 --> 00:02:55.453
then you need to find your bigger stage moment.

00:02:55.756 --> 00:02:58.060
And it may be scary

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.624
it may mean jumping out of your comfort zone

00:03:00.624 --> 00:03:02.928
it may mean doing things that you haven’t done before

00:03:03.513 --> 00:03:05.204
but if you want to have a real impact

00:03:05.822 --> 00:03:08.124
if you want to really build a significant business

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:09.943
if you want to look back on your life and say

00:03:09.943 --> 00:03:10.925
“Yeah, I did that.

00:03:10.925 --> 00:03:13.362
I’m proud of the mark that I left on this world”

00:03:15.177 --> 00:03:16.697
then you need to embrace the fear

00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:18.197
go with it anyway

00:03:19.077 --> 00:03:21.361
and find your bigger stage opportunity.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:22.835
Believe.

